      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is the liver disease of this century, increasing in parallel with obesity, insulin resistance and the metabolic syndrome. NAFLD can be seen as a component of the metabolic syndrome, and as such, contributing as a risk factor for cardiovascular disease. In fact, these patients die more often from cardiovascular disease than from direct consequences of liver disease. In this review, we will summarize the data that link NAFLD as a central player in this dysmetabolism, as well as the evidence for appropriate therapy, in order to improve not only liver disease prognosis, but also the overall prognosis and risk of mortality, with particular focus on cardiovascular risk."},
